damage: free the screen private after the downstream CloseScreen, not before

damageCloseScreen() freed its screen private and then called the
downstream CloseScreen, but layers wrapped BELOW damage still call back
into it while they close. In particular the software cursor's
miSpriteCloseScreen (mi/misprite.c) calls DamageUnregister, which does
damageScrPriv(pScreen) and dereferences that freed allocation.

On the Pi 4 glamor server this crashed every time the desktop session was
closed: exiting the window manager ran xlaunch's teardown, and
Xphoenix-glamor-daemon took a Data Abort reading at 0x60 -- x0, the
privates address handed to dixGetPrivate, was &((base)NULL)->devPrivates,
with the caller chain DamageUnregister -> dixLookupPrivate ->
dixGetPrivate.

Defer the free until the downstream chain has finished unwinding. Nothing
else in this function depends on the private after the unwraps, so the
ordering change is confined to the lifetime of the allocation itself.

--- a/miext/damage/damage.c
+++ b/miext/damage/damage.c
@@ -1595,14 +1595,24 @@
 static Bool
 damageCloseScreen(ScreenPtr pScreen)
 {
+    Bool ret;
+
     damageScrPriv(pScreen);
 
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, DestroyPixmap);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CreateGC);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CopyWindow);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CloseScreen);
+
+    /* Free the screen private only AFTER the downstream CloseScreen returns.
+     * Layers wrapped below damage still call back into it while they close: the
+     * software cursor's miSpriteCloseScreen calls DamageUnregister (mi/misprite.c),
+     * which does damageScrPriv(pScreen) and dereferences this very allocation.
+     * Freeing it first left that a use-after-free. */
+    ret = (*pScreen->CloseScreen) (pScreen);
     free(pScrPriv);
-    return (*pScreen->CloseScreen) (pScreen);
+
+    return ret;
 }
 
 /**
